{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you've studied any physics you know that\nlike charges repel and opposite charges attract."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5680,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But why?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7040,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's as though this thing - electric charge\n- is as fundamental a property of an object"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11440,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as its mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12800,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It just sort of ... exists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well it turns out if you dig deep enough,\nthe fundamental-ness of charge unravels, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20880,
    "dDurationMs": 8960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in many things, including mass itself,\nunravel with it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30880,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although many mysteries remain in physics,\nat least our understanding of electricity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34960,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and magnetism seems pretty complete."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The math that describes it - Maxwell's equations \nor quantum electrodynamics - seem to wrap"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43360,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it up nicely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except that all of electromagnetism is powered\nby a single property: electric charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And neither Maxwell’s equations nor QED\nsay a thing about what electric charge really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56240,
    "dDurationMs": 240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57200,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It seems to just be a property that particles\ncan have or not have - as fundamental as mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like we’ve got to the exasperated end of\na child’s train of but why?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70080,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But why?”, where the only answer is: \"just\nbecause.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea of “fundamental” feels like the\nphysics version of “just because”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80720,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But actually, in the case of electric charge\nwe have at least one or two more “but why’s”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87520,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with which we can annoy the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90240,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we're going to ask them, \nand the answers will take us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94560,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the birth of Particle Physics,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96240,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and, in fact, through the birth of the universe\nitself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As with much of modern physics, this story\nbegins with Werner Heisenberg, whose epiphanies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105120,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "birthed quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At some point, Heisenberg turned his remarkable \nintellect to the newly discovered neutron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He was suspicious of its similarity to the\nproton - they’re practically twins in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117360,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atomic nucleus, occurring with similar numbers\nand almost the same mass, with the only major"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122720,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "difference being our mysterious friend - electric\ncharge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126400,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The neutron seemed like a chargeless, \nor neutral proton, hence the name."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Heisenberg wondered if the two particles were\nin fact just different states of a single"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135600,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle which he called the nucleon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At this point we already knew of particles\nthat had internal states."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143760,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example electrons have this thing called\nspin - a quantum analogy to angular momentum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Spin can take on discrete values; in the electrons \nit can be +1/2 or -1/2, loosely corresponding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the spin axis being aligned or anti-aligned\nwith your measurement device, which we’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161200,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "call “up” and “down” states."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163680,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And spin is conserved - flip an electron’s\nspin and the difference has to be transferred"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168560,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by a photon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if the protons and neutrons are just two\nstates of the same particle, Heisenberg reasoned"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175680,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that they may be differentiated by a property "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178640,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "analogous to spin, governed \nby analogous mathematics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thus, in 1932 Heisenberg proposed a \nnew fundamental property of matter:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188320,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Isospin, a contraction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190400,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of isotopic or isobaric spin, depending on\nwho you ask."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this theory the proton would be the “up”\nstate with isospin 1/2, and the neutron would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199920,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be the down state with isospin -1/2."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203360,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By introducing this new conserved quantity, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205760,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Heisenberg started to make \nsense of the relationship"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208160,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between protons and neutrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, with this choice of their relative\nisospins, it made sense why nuclei prefered"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to have roughly equal numbers of protons and\nneutrons, and at the same time allowed precise"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220640,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "predictions of the outcome of \ncollisions between these particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for isospin to really do its job, it needed\nto explain the most obvious difference between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229600,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protons and neutrons - which is to say electric\ncharge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Charge would have to depend on isospin, which\ncould mean that charge is not a fundamental"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239200,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "property after all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240960,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fast forward a few decades."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our particle colliders advanced, leading to\nthe discovery of weird new particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247760,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So many of them, in fact, that physicists\nstruggled to make sense of this so-called"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252080,
    "dDurationMs": 800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle zoo."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253520,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there were some clues."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, some of these particles had very\nsimilar masses but very different electric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259760,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charges, which I hope reminds you of the proton\nand neutron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So maybe each of these groups were really\na single particle in different states - with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269200,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different isospins."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270640,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The case for isospin was solidifying."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273360,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what exactly was the connection between\nisospin and electric charge?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well that mystery was solved independently\nby Kazuhiko Nishijima and Murray Gell-Mann."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Peering into the depths of the particle zoo,\nthey noticed another pattern."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There seemed to be a family of particles that\nwere only created in pairs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294320,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Similar to how the electron and positron are\nonly created in pairs in order to conserve"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299200,
    "dDurationMs": 880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electric charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these new particles weren’t doing this\nto conserve charge, nor isospin, nor any other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306720,
    "dDurationMs": 880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "known property."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This suggested a brand new conserved \nquantity, which was altogether strange."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the same way that isospin followed the\nsame mathematics as regular quantum spin,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318720,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this new property seemed to obey the math\nfor our old friend electric charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323600,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They called it Hypercharge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325920,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nishijima and Gell-Mann discovered an even\ndeeper pattern."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electric charge, isospin and hypercharge were\nintimately connected across all particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337520,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, it seemed that electric charge was\njust isospin plus half of hypercharge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To be pedantic, that’s the z-component of\nisospin, but we’ll put that complication"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349520,
    "dDurationMs": 800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aside for now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350880,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The conservation of fundamental properties\ndefines which interactions are possible and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355840,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which are impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357680,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Charge alone couldn’t explain the patterns\nof interactions and particle types observed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362560,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the particle zoo."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364080,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However hypercharge and isospin seemed to\ndo a much better job - suggesting that these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370080,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may in fact be more fundamental than charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373040,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there remained a mystery."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not every combination of isospin \nand hypercharge were possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379440,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was Murray Gell-Mann who first noticed\nthis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Plotting particles according to their isospin\nand hypercharge revealed peculiar geometric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388720,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patterns."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example some groups of eight particles\nformed hexagons, and one group of ten particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396160,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formed a triangle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397680,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except that the triangle was missing the bottom\ncorner."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No big deal - Gell-Mann just hypothesized\nan undiscovered particle - the omega baryon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407360,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- with the right isospin and hypercharge to\nfill that hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sort of like how Mendeleev had used holes\nin the Periodic Table to predict the existence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417360,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of unknown elements."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419120,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And when the omega was \ndiscovered by experimentalists, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423200,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gell-Mann got his Nobel prize."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425680,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Isospin and hypercharge seemed to be “deeper”\nthan electric charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the geometric relationship between these\ntwo new properties hinted that there may exist"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even deeper physics yet; even more \nfundamental rules which explained why they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441200,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should be constrained in these ways."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444240,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again, it was Gell-Mann who figured this out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He recognized that these patterns were actually\nrepresentations of a mathematical symmetry"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452720,
    "dDurationMs": 1173,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "known as SU(3)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unfortunately we can’t get into the gory\ndetails of symmetry groups in this episode,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but in short, Gell-Mann realized that he could \nmake sense of the geometric symmetry if nucleons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466160,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "themselves were not elementary particles,\nbut rather made up of smaller components,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471360,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which he dubbed quarks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He showed that isospin and hypercharge were\njust emergent properties that reflected the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479040,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different types of quarks that make up one\nof these particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Starting with experiments at the Stanford\nLinear Accelerator Center in 1968, the reality"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488720,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of quarks quickly became conclusive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So after all this hard thinking it turns out\nthat isospin and hypercharge were as much"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498080,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mathematical abstractions as was electric charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501040,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There must be something deeper - something\nthat lives in the hearts of these quarks and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505280,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other elementary particles - that governs\nthese differences between particle groups,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509440,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that also governs electric charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The quark model for nucleons led to a description \nof the strong nuclear force via this SU(3)"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517440,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stuff to give us quantum chromodynamics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520400,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that’s a story for another time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522880,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may seem like the strong force led us astray\n- but actually it points to the answer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing, the early approaches of Heisenberg \nand Gell-Mann and others are exactly what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534400,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we need - it's just that they were applied\nto the wrong force of nature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539360,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it's by unraveling one of the forces of\nnature that we can explain electric charge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543920,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- but it's not the strong force, it's not\neven electromagnetism."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The secrets of electric charge are actually\nhiding in the last, most obscure of the quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552640,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forces - the weak force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554960,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s also the weirdest force of all, and "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557680,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we need to consider two of \nits weirdest properties."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560880,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, the weak force can transform particles into \nother particles - something no other force can do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568160,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Second, it only works on left-handed particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572480,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That second really does sound weird, and it\nis - but it's the thing that's going to connect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577040,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of this back to quantum spin, which is\nsort of where we started."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One consequence of quantum spin is this thing\ncalled chirality, which is sort of the projection"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587120,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of spin in the direction that a particle is\nmoving."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589760,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's more complicated, obviously, but that'll\ndo for now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Particles can have right-handed chirality\nif their spin is clockwise relative to their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597520,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "momentum vector and left-handed chirality\nif it's counter-clockwise."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602160,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only particles with left-handed chirality\nfeel the weak force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605680,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the electron has both a right-\nand left-handed component."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610000,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only the left-handed component can emit one\nof the weak-force carrier particles - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615120,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "W boson - and in doing so transform into a\nneutrino."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember that Heisenberg imagined that the\nproton and neutron were differentiated by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624160,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this new conserved quantity, isospin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627440,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can play the same trick with the electron\nand neutrino."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it turns out that the new conserved quantity\nbehaves eerily similarly to isospin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636800,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We're going to call it weak isospin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639920,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only left-handed particles have it, and so\nit has an intimate connection to the quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644880,
    "dDurationMs": 560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645440,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Weak isospin is effectively the charge of\nthe weak force, carried by these W bosons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To fully explain weak interactions we need\na second charge - this one carried by the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657600,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Z boson."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It acts more like electric charge, so we'll\nbe imaginative and call it weak hypercharge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And here's the weird thing: weak isospin and\nweak hypercharge are mixed in exactly the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670560,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same way as Gell-Mann’s versions of these\nquantities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674320,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which is to say, electric charge equals weak\nisospin plus half weak hypercharge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we know these weak versions of isospin\nand hypercharge must be fundamental because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686640,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they are properties of elementary particles\nthat can't be broken into smaller pieces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Particles like the electron, the neutrino,\nand even the quark."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695040,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's right, quarks feel the weak force and\nobey the same rule for their electric charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that our old strong-force versions\nof isospin and hypercharge in the composite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705920,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles of the particle zoo emerge from\ntheir different quark content, but are driven"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712160,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by these more \"real\" weak-force quantities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let me summarize where we've got to: the charge \nthat drives electromagnetism is governed by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721600,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the charges that drive the weak force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724640,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So does that mean that electric charge is\nnot really fundamental?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730160,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We need to question what fundamental really\nmeans."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733120,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What we’ve learned is that electromagnetism\nand the weak force are deeply connected."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738240,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or, should I say, were connected."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740720,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These two forces were once united in what\nwe call the electroweak force, whose charges"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747360,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were the same weak isospin and hypercharge\nthat we just discovered."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Something happened to that force in the very\nearly universe to force these charges to only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take on a specific combination of values - the\ncombination that we now observe as electric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762080,
    "dDurationMs": 320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763360,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That event - the breaking of electroweak symmetry \n- created the weak and electromagnetic forces"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769120,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as we know them today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we now know that electric charge is a sort\nof shadow of the ancient fields from the birth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776320,
    "dDurationMs": 800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Very soon we’ll follow this thread deeper\nto fully understand why these fields separated,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and how, in this process, the Higgs field\nwas also created."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which, by the way, grants mass to elementary\nparticles - yet another supposedly “fundamental”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794320,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "property”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795360,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But is it any more fundamental that the \ndubiously fundamental electric charge?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800880,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if mass isn't fundamental, then what is?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804800,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll find out soon as we continue to unravel\nthe tangled symmetries of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just a couple of things before we go: FIrst we’d\nlike to tell you about Subcultured, a brand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new documentary series from PBS that explores \nthe lesser known communities and folx that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have had a major impact on the mainstream\nand answers questions like: What can somebody"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826800,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who is completely blind teach you about video\ngames?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830320,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How did anime become a 25 billion dollar industry?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833920,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And why are these guys putting underwear on\ngoats?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can check it out over on PBS Voices, link\nin our description, and let them know (politely)"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842560,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that Space Time sent you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844080,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the second think is just our thanks. Thanks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846720,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for watching. Clicking that link \nwhen you get the notification really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850080,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "helps the show grow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As does subscribing - just tap that little\nred button you see down there - easy peasy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856720,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course if you're feeling extra extra \nsupportive, you could always join us on Patreon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have a really great community - so come\nhang out with us on the discord and on our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865600,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "monthly Zoom chat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867200,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if you're already a Patreon supporter\n- well thank you!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870320,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We're hypercharged to have you with us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But today I want to give an extra special \nthank you to David Taiclet, who's supporting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877600,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us at the big bang level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879360,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "David, the swiss are famous for their chocolatiers \nand for their giant particle colliders."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, we arranged a collaboration and have\nput together a nice box of assorted exotic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889040,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hadron flavoured candy as \na token of our gratitude."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "My favorites are the fudge-filled hexaquarks,\nwhich are one of the most delicious candidates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896960,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the rho meson truffles .. but eat them\nbefore they decay in 10^-24 seconds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904240,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But maybe just skip the chocolate-dipped \nKaons - they taste a little strange."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909440,
    "dDurationMs": 16400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you David, as you can tell, you are\nsupporting some very important work."
    } ]
  } ]
}
